From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 04:20:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] drm/apple: m4: fade between panel backlight levels

The T8132 backend sends the panel level in the swap, and firmware 26.6.2
shows each level at once. Its policy jumps to every new level, so any
change is a visible step. On a MacBook Pro (14-inch, M4) with an encrypted
root this shows on every boot: the first present carries the board
record's 140 nits, the passphrase prompt shows at that level, and after
unlock systemd-backlight restores the saved level (48 nits here) while
the boot splash is still up. The DCP log shows brightness_changed 140.0
and then 48.0 nits (16.16), a hard step on a dark screen 0.7 s before
the splash quits.

Fade there as on H17P. The first present still carries the requested
level at once, as the panel does not show it yet. After that a new level
fades from the level presented last over DCP_BL_FADE_MS, one
brightness-only present per DCP_BL_FADE_STEP_MS at most; steps that
come faster than presents complete merge into the next one, and steps
also ride on the compositor's flips. Blanking, unblanking, suspend and a
level the panel may not show yet still jump. A fade only moves between
two accepted levels, so it never exceeds the requested level nor the
board maximum.

Brightness-only presents on this firmware are the existing backlight
work path; this only makes it run once per step instead of once per
change.

Add a KUnit case for the T8132 setup: the first present carries the
default, the restored level fades down monotonically and ends exactly
at its target, a fade up stops at its target, and a level requested
before the first present completes is presented directly.

Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Assisted-by: LLM
(cherry picked from commit d434b638c87ba7b5a12921cd49568f829174fc43)
[j604: k610 drm sync: dcp_backlight_test.c conflict only (both sides add tests/cases: upstream EDR cases, this T8132 fade case). Upstream 50d5865ea6 still makes the T8132 panel jump (dcp_backlight_configure()); Aurora 2924f21d75 fades the DAC-firmware panels only, so this fade is still needed and its dcp_backlight.c hunk applied unchanged.]
(cherry picked from commit 49001ba992a2ab34f5366985ddfa1b4fb83a7660)
(cherry picked from commit aa32bce92aff368946c6aeb4c94c8cdf86eb936d)
---
 drivers/gpu/drm/apple/dcp_backlight.c | 9 +++++++--
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/apple/dcp_backlight.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/apple/dcp_backlight.c
index 4af8861bdc..47cbfb6384 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/apple/dcp_backlight.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/apple/dcp_backlight.c
@@ -294,10 +294,15 @@ int dcp_backlight_configure(struct apple_dcp *dcp, u32 maximum,
 				  default_valid, default_nits);
 		if (!ret && iomfb_is_m4(dcp)) {
 			/*
-			 * The J713 panel jumps to new levels, and its first
-			 * present carries the requested level.
+			 * The first present carries the requested level, as
+			 * the panel does not show it yet.  The firmware shows
+			 * each present's level at once, so later levels fade
+			 * from the one presented last, as on H17P: otherwise
+			 * the level restored after boot is a visible step on
+			 * the boot splash.
 			 */
 			dcp_bl_jump(&dcp->backlight.state);
+			dcp->backlight.state.fade = true;
 			dcp->backlight.kick = kick;
 		} else if (!ret) {
 			dcp->backlight.state.fade = !iomfb_v14_7_panel_nits(dcp);
